{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAotv7zqos1uDVgSv22R","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've fixed the generator, but it hasn't been tested on the watch: I didn't push anything to Intervals, so we won't know until you send a new event and sync.\n\n**Cause:** `gen.py` wrote each HR range as `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}`. In Intervals' workout format, a range is written with `start` and `end`; `value` means a single target. So Intervals ignored `end` and treated every step as a single %LTHR target. It exports a single HR target to Garmin as a zone number, and the watch then shows its own band for that zone. That explains why every step showed a watch zone, and why changing the watch's LTHR moved the bands without ever giving 151–160.\n\n**Fix (`hr_target` in `gen.py`):** ranges are now written as `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}`. If a plan gives the same low and high value, it writes a single `value` target instead. I regenerated the tempo plan and the output has the new fields.\n\n`ANSWER.md` has the full explanation, including why the earlier `\"bpm\"` and `start_bpm`/`end_bpm` attempts failed.\n\n**To check it:** run `python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json > event.json && ./push.sh event.json`, then sync the watch. It should show about 117–129, 151–160, 129–141 and 112–124 bpm. You may want to delete event 184233 first so the old version doesn't stay on the calendar."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":935,"cache_read_input_tokens":23763,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":935},"output_tokens":487,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f2389841-42ad-481f-ae69-21915de2c51d"